SECTION 1. DEFINITIONS
================================================================================

1.1 "Software" means the BIOME autonomous multi-agent software engineering ecosystem, 
including without limitation: (a) all source code, object code, scripts, and build 
configurations; (b) all system prompts, meta-prompts, persona instructions, and context 
management rules (including the Elephant, Goldfish, Beaver, and Woodpecker agent 
definitions); (c) the Structure, Sequence, and Organization ("SSO"), ephemeral agent 
spawning workflows, context-compaction pipelines, and verification state-machines; and 
(d) all accompanying documentation, schemas, and templates authored by Asteria Studio.

1.2 "Derivative Work" means any work, product, model, system, or adaptation that is 
based upon, derived from, or incorporates all or any portion of the Software, whether 
in literal or non-literal form. This explicitly includes, without limitation: forks, 
translations into other programming or natural languages, semantic paraphrasing of 
agent prompts, architectural clones replicating the Software's SSO, API wrappers, 
containerized distributions, plugins, extensions, or clean-room re-implementations 
conducted by individuals who have accessed the Software.

1.3 "Commercial Hosting or Competitive Exploitation" means any activity in which the 
Software or any Derivative Work is used, directly or indirectly, to:
  (a) offer a hosted, cloud-based, Software-as-a-Service (SaaS), Platform-as-a-Service 
      (PaaS), Agent-as-a-Service (AaaS), or managed service accessible by any Third Party;
  (b) provide commercial API endpoints, remote execution environments, or automated 
      code-generation pipelines to Third Parties;
  (c) bundle, embed, or tie the Software with any paid, subscription-based, ad-supported, 
      token-metered, or compute-metered product, IDE, platform, or developer tool (even 
      if the Software component itself is nominally offered at zero cost);
  (d) operate as an internal execution engine for an agency, consultancy, software house, 
      or outsourcing provider to deliver paid software development, auditing, or 
      engineering services to Third-Party clients; or
  (e) sell, lease, sublicense, rent, or commercially distribute the Software or any 
      Derivative Work as a standalone or integrated commercial product.

1.4 "Third Party" means any individual, corporation, partnership, organization, or 
legal entity other than You, including Your customers, clients, external users, 
unrelated contractors, and separate corporate Affiliates or subsidiaries utilizing 
the Software as a shared service.

